---
import AnswerBlock from "../../components/AnswerBlock.astro";
import { getGuide } from "../../data/pages";
import GuideLayout from "../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro";

const meta = getGuide("/guides/claude-code-codex-usage-monitor-mac");
const faqs = [
  {
    question: "Why does a cost tracker show a different number from /usage?",
    answer:
      "Because they answer different questions. Cost trackers such as ccusage read your local session logs and report what that activity would have cost at API prices. /usage reports how much of your subscription window you have consumed. Neither converts into the other, and a subscription plan has no dollar figure to compare against, so the two numbers will never agree.",
  },
  {
    question: "Which usage number should I trust?",
    answer:
      "The provider's own display, always. Whenever a local view and the provider disagree, the provider is right. Everything a third-party tool adds — multiple accounts at once, reading age, consumption pace — is context around that number, not a replacement for it.",
  },
  {
    question: "Why did my remaining usage go up without me doing anything?",
    answer:
      "The Claude Code session window rolls continuously rather than resetting at a fixed time, so capacity returns gradually as older activity ages out of the window. Two readings a few minutes apart can legitimately differ in your favour. The weekly window has a definite reset point and does not behave this way.",
  },
  {
    question: "How old is too old for a usage reading?",
    answer:
      "Old enough that you would not bet an hour of work on it. Limit Lifeboat marks anything over 30 minutes as stale rather than presenting it as current, and shows the age of every reading. A percentage without an age attached is not enough information to plan a long task around.",
  },
  {
    question: "Does checking usage consume a meaningful part of the limit?",
    answer:
      "No coding prompt or model turn is submitted. Codex readings use the locally installed Codex app server's rate-limit request, while Claude uses provider usage services with a local Claude Code usage fallback.",
  },
  {
    question: "Why can an inactive account show an older reading?",
    answer:
      "A saved login can expire, Keychain may deny background access, or a provider request can fail. Limit Lifeboat keeps the last successful reading and shows its age rather than presenting stale data as live.",
  },
] as const;
---

<GuideLayout
  meta={meta}
  introduction="Run /usage, open a menu-bar monitor, and check a cost tracker, and you will often get three different answers about the same account. None of them is necessarily broken. They measure different things, from different sources, at different moments — and knowing which is which tells you whether you can start a long task."
  faqs={faqs}
  ctaHeading="Know which number is the one that will stop you."
>
  <Fragment slot="nav">
    <a href="#sources">Four tools, four sources</a>
    <a href="#why">Five reasons they disagree</a>
    <a href="#window">Which window actually stops you</a>
    <a href="#stale">Reading age</a>
    <a href="#authoritative">What is authoritative</a>
    <a href="#questions">Questions</a>
    <a href="#related">Related guides</a>
  </Fragment>

  <AnswerBlock
    facts={[
      "Cost trackers report API-equivalent spend; /usage reports subscription window consumption. These are different quantities.",
      "Claude Code reports a roughly 5-hour session window, a weekly all-models window, and weekly windows scoped to individual models.",
      "The session window rolls continuously, so remaining capacity can rise without a reset.",
      "Every tool caches, so a reading without an age attached is not evidence about now.",
      "When a local view and the provider's own display disagree, the provider is right.",
    ]}
  >
    <p>
      Claude Code, Codex CLI, menu-bar monitors and cost trackers disagree about usage because they
      are not measuring the same thing. A cost tracker reads local session logs and reports what the
      activity would have cost at API prices; <code>/usage</code> reports how much of a subscription
      window has been consumed; a menu-bar monitor reports a cached copy of the latter across several
      accounts. The number that actually stops you is the tightest of your current windows, as the
      provider reports it.
    </p>
  </AnswerBlock>

  <section id="sources">
    <h2>Four tools, four sources</h2>
    <p>
      Almost every disagreement resolves once you know where each number came from.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong><code>/usage</code> inside Claude Code or Codex CLI</strong> — asks the provider and
        reports plan limits and rate-limit status for the account the CLI is currently authenticated
        as. Authoritative, and scoped to one account.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The provider's own web dashboard</strong> — the same underlying data, for whichever
        account the browser is signed into. That is frequently a <em>different</em> account from the
        one your CLI is using, which is a common source of apparent contradiction; see
        <a href="/guides/check-active-claude-code-account">checking which account is active</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Cost trackers such as ccusage</strong> — read local session logs and compute what the
        recorded activity would have cost at published API rates. This is a genuinely useful number
        and it is not a quota percentage. On a subscription there is no dollar budget for it to be a
        percentage of.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Menu-bar monitors</strong> — poll the provider on a schedule and cache the result, so
        they can show several accounts at once. Their number is the provider's number, delayed by
        however long ago the poll ran.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="why">
    <h2>Five reasons the numbers disagree</h2>
    <h3>1. They are answering different questions</h3>
    <p>
      Spend and quota are not convertible. A cost tracker telling you that yesterday was worth $47 of
      API usage says nothing about how much of your weekly window remains, because the subscription
      does not meter in dollars. Both numbers are correct; only one of them can stop you working.
    </p>
    <h3>2. They are reading different windows</h3>
    <p>
      Claude Code reports several windows at once: a rolling session window of about five hours, a
      weekly all-models window, and weekly windows scoped to individual model families. A tool that
      displays only one of them will look wrong to anyone watching a different one. Codex windows are
      labelled from the durations OpenAI reports rather than a fixed pair, and some plans expose only
      a weekly window.
    </p>
    <h3>3. The session window rolls</h3>
    <p>
      The five-hour window is not a bucket that empties at a fixed time. It rolls, so older activity
      ages out continuously and remaining capacity climbs back on its own. Two readings taken minutes
      apart can legitimately differ, and remaining usage going <em>up</em> while you sit still is
      expected behaviour rather than a bug.
    </p>
    <h3>4. One of them is cached</h3>
    <p>
      Everything that shows several accounts at once is showing you cached data, because polling the
      provider continuously for every saved account is neither free nor kind. The question is not
      whether a tool caches — they all do — but whether it tells you how stale the number is.
    </p>
    <h3>5. They are looking at different accounts</h3>
    <p>
      The most common cause and the easiest to miss. Your browser, your desktop app and your CLI each
      hold their own session, and the CLI's identity is the only one that governs what the CLI
      spends.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="window">
    <h2>Which window actually stops you</h2>
    <p>
      The tightest one. That is the whole rule, and it is why a single headline percentage is
      misleading: 60% remaining on the session window is irrelevant if the weekly all-models window
      is at 4%.
    </p>
    <p>
      A percentage on its own is also not enough to plan around. What makes a reading actionable is
      the combination of the number, the reset time, and the pace — how fast the current window is
      being consumed relative to how much time is left on it. Forty percent remaining is comfortable
      on a Monday and a problem on a Friday afternoon if the last hour consumed fifteen points.
    </p>
    <p>
      For what each Claude window measures and how each one rolls over, see
      <a href="/guides/claude-code-usage-limits-explained">Claude Code usage limits explained</a>;
      for the Codex equivalents and earned rate-limit resets, see
      <a href="/guides/codex-cli-rate-limits-explained">Codex CLI rate limits explained</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="stale">
    <h2>Reading age is a feature, not a detail</h2>
    <p>
      A tool that shows a cached percentage as though it were live is worse than one that shows
      nothing, because it invites you to commit an hour of work to a number from half an hour ago.
    </p>
    <p>
      Limit Lifeboat refreshes every 5 minutes by default, adjustable to 2, 5, 10, 15, 30 or 60
      minutes, and accelerates to every minute when an active account reaches 90% session usage or
      94% weekly usage — even when automatic switching is off. Any reading older than 30 minutes is
      marked stale rather than displayed as current, and an account whose saved login has failed
      keeps its last successful reading with the age attached instead of silently showing a number it
      cannot justify. Local usage history is retained for 30 days, up to 5,000 records per account,
      and exports to CSV with identity fields such as emails and organization names deliberately
      omitted.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="authoritative">
    <h2>What is authoritative, and where readings come from</h2>
    <p>
      Whenever a local view and the provider's own display disagree, the provider is right. Provider
      limits also change, and consumption is workload-dependent — task size, complexity, model and
      where the task runs all move it.
    </p>
    <p>
      In Claude Code, run <code>/usage</code> to view plan limits and rate-limit status. In Codex CLI,
      <code>/usage</code> reports account token activity and available rate-limit resets. OpenAI also
      directs users approaching a Codex limit to the Codex usage page or the in-product limit banner;
      see <a href="https://help.openai.com/en/articles/11369540-using-codex-with-your-chatgpt-plan"
        >Using Codex with your ChatGPT plan</a
      > and the app-server documentation for <a
        href="https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/app-server#8-earned-rate-limit-resets-chatgpt"
        >earned rate-limit resets</a
      >.
    </p>
    <pre><code># Inside Claude Code
/usage

# Inside Codex CLI
/usage</code></pre>
    <p>
      Limit Lifeboat fetches Claude usage with the saved account's OAuth credentials, falling back to
      Claude Code's local usage view. Codex usage and earned-reset redemption use the locally
      installed Codex app server with an isolated temporary credential copy for the selected account;
      token updates are saved only if no newer external CLI login replaced them. Earned resets are
      provider-issued entitlements reported and enforced by OpenAI — they can be displayed and
      redeemed, not created, and they do not bypass a limit.
    </p>
    <p>
      Profiles and history stay under your user Library. Credential snapshots are encrypted by the
      macOS Keychain and are neither synced to iCloud nor included in device backups; during a switch,
      short-lived rollback files are protected by filesystem permissions rather than Keychain
      encryption, and are removed once the switch commits or rolls back. The exact storage locations,
      network destinations and permissions are in the <a href="/privacy">privacy documentation</a>,
      and <a href="/support">support</a> lists the checks to run when a reading looks wrong.
    </p>
    <p>
      Once you trust the reading, the decision it feeds is which account to work on. That is covered
      in <a href="/guides/claude-code-limit-reached">what to do when the limit is reached</a>, and the
      mechanics in <a href="/guides/switch-claude-code-accounts-mac">switching Claude Code accounts</a>
      and <a href="/guides/manage-multiple-codex-accounts-mac">using multiple Codex CLI accounts</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>
